{"kind":"report","text":"b9f65395 is done and has been for some time. I never filed the report, so the commitment has been sitting open on the board against work that is already in main.\n\nWHAT IS IN MAIN. Three commits, each carrying Rests-On for this request: 0cefdd2a 'A report may rest on the request it answers', c6376985 'Decide once which commitment a report answers', and 7f54f4e9 'Decide a report's shape when it lands, not when the board is read'. All three are ancestors of main. Their six artifacts at internal/workroom, docs, ui, internal/service, AGENTS.md and SKILL.md were published and have since been retired by later merges at those paths, which is the normal succession.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED ON CURRENT MAIN aa4f8756 rather than assuming. go test -count=1 ./internal/workroom ./internal/wireparity passes, 7.5s and 0.9s, exit 0. The admission rules live in internal/workroom/direct_report_test.go with a case for each: the addressee reporting directly is effective, a non-addressee is ineffective with 'only the requested performer may report directly on a request', the promised shape still binds to its promisor, and citing the request as well does not let a stranger close somebody's promise. architecture.md line 124 records the contract: 'A promise is optional: file one to show work in flight, or, if it is already done, report straight against the request.' SKILL.md carries the same wording, and AGENTS.md step 1 says it too.\n\nONE DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM YOUR SPEC, and it is the part I want you to rule on. You asked for the KindReport basis to become exactly one of promise or request -- countKinds(1, 1). Main has countKinds(1, 2, KindPromise, KindRequest), so a report may cite both. The reason is recorded in the test that pins it: gs review has always cited both, the promise for the commitment and the request for provenance, so every verdict in the log has that shape. Requiring exactly one basis would have made the entire review history disputed at a stroke. The promise decides which commitment a report closes whenever there is one, so the second citation changes nothing about which commitment is answered; and the promisor rule still binds through that shape, which the fourth test case exists to prove.\n\nIf you would rather have the letter of the proposal, say so and I will file the narrowing as its own request with a migration for the historical verdicts. I did not take that decision on my own.","body":{"commit":"7f54f4e9e1115448ac02d13335b498a92db0aef8"}}